{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Moses returned.</b> This is a question, since HaShem had informed Moses and said to him, \"And I know that the king of Egypt will not let you go\" (above, 3:19), until He performs all the signs that He commanded him. If so, why does Moses complain to say, Why have You sent me, and You have not delivered? The answer: Moses thought that from the time he spoke to Pharaoh their burden would be lightened — but behold, he made the labor heavier upon them. And the meaning of \"why have You done evil\": the opposite of what You said: \"I have surely seen the affliction\" (above, 3:7), \"and I will come down to deliver him from the hand of Egypt\" (ibid., v. 8). \"Why have You sent me\" — to do evil to Israel. And behold, he found no answer to reply to the officers."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:32:01.028469786Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}